  "qualifications": [
    "The initial 95-module run passed 92 modules, found two existing stale/Windows fixtures, and reached the 1500-second timeout in the default 15-km drive module. That long default route was not completed.",
    "The CLI golden and mocked container-path fixtures were corrected and passed. A starting-commit baseline reproduced the Windows prune-audit failure. Drive checks now normalize paths, stop follow-event attribution at a rebase coordinate-frame change, and wait for test-server descendant log handles during Windows teardown.",
    "After the final production changes, all 12 affected Python modules passed (227 tests), all 61 Lua suites passed, and the two remaining drive cases passed. The other successful module/drive logs are retained from the broader runs.",
    "35 Python skips cover Linux-only checks, unavailable game/GPU/resource/FIFO tools, standalone LuaJIT discovery, and optional gravity-download/timing opt-ins. Lua was also tested separately through Lupa. No skip is represented as a passed assertion.",
    "Benchmarks are synthetic offline measurements over nine fixtures and all four backend combinations, seven repeats, real netconvert, and fresh terrain builds. No real-world screenshots, AI behavior, game frame times, Linux smoke run, or frozen-SUMO packaging validation is claimed.",
    "Neither BeamNG.drive nor the SUMO simulator was launched or controlled. Legacy defaults and original/Group 1 requirement files are unchanged."
  ],
